<p>Corpus Christi hurricane damage claims are rarely simple. A single storm can tear off roofing, drive rain through walls and windows, push surge across the bays and barrier islands, and leave a property owner dealing with two or three insurers at once. If you own a home or business in Corpus Christi, Nueces County, or the surrounding Coastal Bend, this page explains how hurricane claims usually work, where disputes tend to start, and how a licensed public adjuster may help.</p>
<p>The Coastal Bend has a long hurricane record. Hurricane Celia came ashore near Aransas Pass on August 3, 1970 as a Category 4 storm (<a href="https://www.weather.gov/crp/hurricanecelia" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NWS Corpus Christi</a>). Hurricane Harvey made landfall just east of Rockport on August 25, 2017, also at Category 4 (<a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/AL092017_Harvey.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NHC Tropical Cyclone Report</a>), and NOAA storm records describe storm tides of 6 to 8 feet in Port Aransas. Hurricane Hanna in 2020 and Tropical Storm Harold in 2023 were smaller, but both produced wind, rain, and elevated tides across the area. Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page covers more of this record.</p>

<h2>How Corpus Christi Hurricane Damage Claims Are Structured</h2>
<p>Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties all sit inside the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) catastrophe area. Because many private insurers exclude wind and hail near the coast, a typical Coastal Bend property owner may hold three separate policies after a hurricane:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>What it usually covers</th><th>Common hurricane issue</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
