<tr><td>TWIA windstorm policy</td><td>Wind and hail only, for eligible properties in the catastrophe area</td><td>Disputes over whether damage came from wind or from water</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td><td>Rising water, including storm surge, subject to policy terms and limits</td><td>Surge damage mixed with wind damage on the same structure</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Each policy has its own deductible, its own deadlines, and its own adjuster. That is where many hurricane claims start to go sideways. For a deeper look at the TWIA side, see our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>Wind vs. Water: The Most Common Hurricane Dispute</h2>
<p>When a home on Padre Island, in Rockport, or along Ocean Drive takes both wind and surge, the wind insurer and the flood insurer may each point to the other. A wall that failed under wind pressure and then flooded can look, after the fact, like a flood-only loss. Interior water stains can come from wind-driven rain through a damaged roof or from rising water. Separating the two is detailed work.</p>
<p>Evidence that often helps includes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Water lines and debris lines</strong> inside and outside the building, measured and photographed.</li>
<li><strong>Roof and opening damage</strong> that shows how rain got in from above.</li>
<li><strong>Tide and surge records</strong> from NOAA stations such as Packery Channel, Port Aransas, or Rockport, plus NWS storm reports.</li>
<li><strong>Timeline evidence</strong>, such as when wind peaked compared with when water arrived.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage claims</a> goes into this in more depth, and the <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> page covers the flood policy side.</p>

