<h2>What Hurricane Damage Often Looks Like on the Coastal Bend</h2>
<p>Construction around Corpus Christi shapes how hurricane damage appears. Slab-on-grade homes with composition shingles are common on the mainland, metal roofs are popular in rural Nueces and San Patricio counties, and many island homes are elevated. Damage we often see documented includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Missing, creased, or lifted shingles and displaced ridge caps; see <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</li>
<li>Wind-driven rain entering through soffits, vents, window frames, and stucco or brick veneer.</li>
<li>Broken windows, garage doors pushed in, and damaged fences, carports, and outbuildings.</li>
<li>Interior ceiling, insulation, drywall, and flooring damage after the roof was opened up.</li>
<li>Surge damage to lower levels, breakaway walls, and equipment on elevated homes.</li>
<li>Follow-on problems such as mold growth when drying was delayed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Deductibles, Depreciation, and Code Upgrades</h2>
<p>Several policy features control what a hurricane claim actually pays:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Named-storm or wind/hail deductibles.</strong> Often a percentage of the dwelling limit. Read more in <a href="/named-storm-hurricane-deductibles-texas/">named-storm and hurricane deductibles in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>ACV vs. RCV.</strong> Under replacement cost coverage, the insurer typically pays actual cash value first and releases recoverable depreciation after repairs, within the time the policy allows.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law coverage.</strong> The City of Corpus Christi adopted the 2021 International Residential Code with local amendments effective August 1, 2023, and re-roofing requires a permit. Code-required upgrades are only paid if your policy includes ordinance or law coverage, up to its limit. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Windstorm certification.</strong> Repairs in the catastrophe area may need TDI windstorm inspection to keep the property eligible for TWIA coverage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Living Expenses, Contents, and Business Income</h2>
<p>The building is only one part of a hurricane claim. If a home is unlivable, many homeowners policies include loss of use or additional living expense coverage for reasonable extra costs such as a rental or hotel, subject to limits and policy terms. Contents claims need a room-by-room inventory with descriptions, ages, and replacement prices. Business owners may also have business income or extra expense coverage, which usually requires financial records showing what the shutdown cost. Each of these pieces is easy to overlook while the focus is on the roof. Our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page covers the business side.</p>

<h2>How a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>As a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster, we represent the policyholder, not the insurance company. On a hurricane claim, that work may include reviewing each policy, inspecting the property, separating wind damage from water damage as far as the evidence allows, preparing a detailed line-item estimate (often in Xactimate), gathering weather and tide records, and handling communication with each insurer's adjuster. If a claim has already been paid low, we can look at whether an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> or appraisal may fit.</p>
<p>We cannot promise a result. Outcomes depend on your policies and the facts of the loss. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take After a Hurricane</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe first.</strong> Wait for local officials to clear your area before returning.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything</strong> before cleanup, including water lines and roof damage.</li>
<li><strong>Report the loss</strong> to every insurer that may be involved: homeowners, TWIA, and flood.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent further damage</strong> with tarps, board-up, and water removal, and keep the receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a claim log</strong> of every call, email, inspection, and payment.</li>
<li><strong>Watch your deadlines.</strong> TWIA claims have statutory deadlines, and other policies set their own. Our <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> can help you track them.</li>
</ol>
<p>Hurricane claims are long and detailed, and many owners are also dealing with temporary housing, contractors, and lost business income at the same time. A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can take on the claim paperwork so you can focus on getting the property back in shape. If you have questions about a lawsuit, bad faith, or the deadline to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
